## [ISSUE] `canManage()` grants manage when the admin only has `view` on all groups

**Severity:** HIGH: privilege escalation. A view-only admin can create top-level groups and reparent existing groups.

### The Problem

```java
@Override
public boolean canManage() {
    if (root.hasOneAdminRole(AdminRoles.MANAGE_USERS)) {
        return true;
    }

    return hasPermission(null, AdminPermissionsSchema.VIEW, AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE);   // line 70
}
```

`hasPermission` returns `true` if **any** of the listed scopes is granted. That means an all-groups permission with only the `view` scope makes `canManage()` true. This looks like a copy-paste of `canView()` (line 52).

### Theory / Proof

- `GroupsResource.addTopLevelGroup` is guarded only by `auth.groups().requireManage()` (GroupsResource.java:181), and that calls `canManage()`.
- The same endpoint also handles `rep.getId() != null`: it looks up an existing group and calls `realm.moveGroup(child, null)`. A view-only admin can therefore detach any group from its parent, not just create new ones. That changes the group hierarchy, and the hierarchy drives `canViewByGroup` / `canManageByGroup` inheritance.
- Every other V2 manage check requires `MANAGE` alone. `canManage(GroupModel)` (line 78) uses `hasPermission(group.getId(), MANAGE)`, and `UserPermissionsV2.canManage(user)` uses only `MANAGE`. So the global check is the only one that accepts `VIEW`.
- The new javadoc in `GroupPermissionEvaluator#canManage()` says "For V2 only: Also if it has permission to VIEW or MANAGE groups", so the bug is now written into the contract too.

### What happens today vs. what will break

`testManageAllGroups` only grants `MANAGE`, and `testViewGroups` never tries a write, so no test catches this. Scenario: create an all-groups permission with scope `view` for admin X. X calls `POST /admin/realms/{realm}/groups` and gets 201 instead of 403.

### Research

V1 `GroupPermissions.canManage()` only checks the `manage-users` role. In Keycloak's FGAP model, `view` never implies `manage`; it is always the other way round (see `canView` checking `VIEW, MANAGE`).

### Recommendation

```java
@Override
public boolean canManage() {
    if (root.hasOneAdminRole(AdminRoles.MANAGE_USERS)) {
        return true;
    }

    return hasPermission(null, AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE);
}
```

Also fix the javadoc in `GroupPermissionEvaluator#canManage()`. Add a test assertion to `testViewGroups`: after granting all-groups `VIEW`, `groups().add(...)` must still return 403.

## [ISSUE] `getGroupIdsWithViewPermission()` returns authz resource IDs instead of group IDs, which breaks user search and count

**Severity:** HIGH: admins granted `view-members`/`manage-members` on all groups get an empty user list and a count of 0.

### The Problem

```java
resourceStore.findByType(server, AdminPermissionsSchema.GROUPS_RESOURCE_TYPE, groupResource -> {
    if (hasPermission(groupResource.getId(), AdminPermissionsSchema.VIEW_MEMBERS, AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE_MEMBERS)) {
        granted.add(groupResource.getId());   // line 123
    }
});
```

There are three separate defects:

1. **Wrong identifier.** `groupResource.getId()` is the authorization `Resource` UUID. In V2 the group ID is stored as the resource **name** (`AdminPermissionsSchema.getOrCreateResource` -> `resolveGroup` returns `group.getId()` and passes it to `resourceStore.create(resourceServer, name, ...)`). V1 gets this right: `granted.add(resource.getName().substring(RESOURCE_NAME_PREFIX.length()))`.
2. **Wrong lookup in `hasPermission`.** `hasPermission(groupResource.getId(), ...)` calls `resourceStore.findByName(server, groupId)` with a resource UUID. That never matches, so the code always falls back to the all-groups type resource.
3. **`findByType` never sees per-group resources.** `getOrCreateResource` creates per-group resources without calling `setType`. Only the `Groups` resource created in `AdminPermissionsSchema.init` (`resource.setType(type)`) has type `Groups`. The JPA query `findResourceIdByType` (`r.type = :type`, ResourceEntity.java:64) therefore returns only that one resource.


**Additional blocker for the V2 path:** even with correct group IDs, `JpaUserProvider.groupsWithPermissionsSubquery` (JpaUserProvider.java:1059-1090) also requires a `ResourceEntity` whose name matches `"group.resource." + groupId`. That is the V1 naming scheme. V2 per-group resources are named with the bare group ID, so the `UserModel.GROUPS` filter can never match anything under V2. The fix therefore also has to make that subquery aware of V2 naming (or skip it when V2 is active), not only return the right IDs.

### What happens today vs. what will break

- **Per-group `view-members` only (the tested case):** the loop finds only the `Groups` type resource. There is no all-groups policy, so `granted` is empty, `UserModel.GROUPS` is not set, and `UsersResource.searchForUser` falls back to loading all users and filtering with `canView`. The result looks correct, but `first`/`max` are applied before the filter, so pages come back short or empty. `getUsersCount` calls `getUsersCount(realm, emptySet)` and does not reflect the visible users.
- **All-groups `view-members`:** `granted = { <UUID of the "Groups" authz resource> }`. `UsersResource.searchForUser` sets `session.setAttribute(UserModel.GROUPS, granted)`. The JPA user search then restricts to members of a group with that UUID, and no such group exists. The admin sees **no users**, even though `canView(user)` (via `canViewByGroup` -> `canViewMembers`, which falls back to the type resource) would return `true` for every group member. `GET /users/count` returns 0 for the same reason. `BruteForceUsersResource` is affected the same way.

`GroupResourceTypeEvaluationTest` only covers per-group permissions, so neither path is exercised.

### Research

V1 `GroupPermissions.getGroupIdsWithViewPermission()` looks up typed resources (`"Group"`) whose name encodes the group ID and strips the prefix. The V2 port needs the same ID-to-name mapping, and it also has to account for V2 resources being untyped.

### Recommendation

Enumerate per-group resources by name, and treat an all-groups grant as "no group restriction":

```java
@Override
public Set<String> getGroupIdsWithViewPermission() {
    if (root.users().canView()) return Collections.emptySet();
    if (!root.isAdminSameRealm()) return Collections.emptySet();

    ResourceServer server = root.realmResourceServer();
    if (server == null) return Collections.emptySet();

    Set<String> granted = new HashSet<>();

    // per-group resources are named by the group id and have no type
    policyStore.findByResourceType(server, AdminPermissionsSchema.GROUPS_RESOURCE_TYPE).stream()
        .flatMap(p -> p.getResources().stream())
        .map(Resource::getName)
        .filter(name -> !AdminPermissionsSchema.GROUPS_RESOURCE_TYPE.equals(name))
        .distinct()
        .filter(groupId -> hasPermission(groupId, AdminPermissionsSchema.VIEW_MEMBERS, AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE_MEMBERS))
        .forEach(granted::add);

    return granted;
}
```

Also handle the all-groups `view-members` case explicitly, either by returning every group ID or by letting the caller skip the `UserModel.GROUPS` restriction. Add a test for all-groups `VIEW_MEMBERS` + `users().search()` + `users().count()`.

## [GAP ANALYSIS] Groups resource type is not handled in `getResourceName`

**Severity:** LOW: the admin UI and REST API show permissions for groups without a readable name.

### The Problem

This PR adds `GROUPS_RESOURCE_TYPE` to `getOrCreateResource` (`case GROUPS_RESOURCE_TYPE -> name = resolveGroup(session, id);`). Per-group resources are created with the group ID as their name and no display name. `getResourceName` (used by `PolicyResourceService` to set `displayName` for each resource of a permission) only handles Users:

```java
if (USERS.getType().equals(resourceType)) {
    if (resource.getName().equals(USERS_RESOURCE_TYPE)) return "All users";
    ...
    return user.getUsername();
}
return resource.getDisplayName();   // null for groups
```

So `GET .../policy/{id}/resources` for a Groups permission returns `displayName == null`, both for per-group resources and for the all-groups resource.

### Recommendation

```java
if (GROUPS.getType().equals(resourceType)) {
    if (resource.getName().equals(GROUPS_RESOURCE_TYPE)) {
        return "All groups";
    }
    GroupModel group = session.groups().getGroupById(session.getContext().getRealm(), resource.getName());
    if (group == null) {
        throw new ModelIllegalStateException("Group not found for resource [" + resource.getId() + "]");
    }
    return group.getName();
}
```
